|passage=“One old boy started '''riding''' me about not having gone to Vietnam; I just spit my coffee at him, and he backed off.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of clothing: to gradually [[move]] (up) and [[crease]]; to [[ruckle]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Ann Kessel|journal=The Guardian|date=27 Jul 2008|titleurl=http://www.guardian.co.uk/sport/2008/jul/27/3
|passage=In athletics, triple jumper Ashia Hansen advises a thong for training because, while knickers '''ride''' up, ‘thongs have nowhere left to go’: but in Beijing Britain's best are likely, she says, to forgo knickers altogether, preferring to go commando for their country under their GB kit.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[rely]], [[depend]] (on). {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Grappling with deficits|journal=The Economist|date=9 Mar 2006
|passage=With so much '''riding''' on the new payments system, it was thus a grave embarrassment to the government when the tariff for 2006-07 had to be withdrawn for amendments towards the end of February.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of clothing: to [[rest]] (in a given way on a part of the body). {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Jenny Eliscu|title=Oops...she's doing it again|journal=The Observer|date=16 Sep 2001|titleurl=http://www.guardian.co.uk/theobserver/2001/sep/16/life1.lifemagazine6
|passage=She's wearing inky-blue jeans that '''ride''' low enough on her hips that her aquamarine thong peeks out teasingly at the back.}}
# {{lb|en|lacrosse}} To play [[defense]] on the defensemen or midfielders, as an attackman.
